Interesting. Your post got me thinking. I think that one of the big
problems of Gehennom is that it has a "more of the same" feel; we've
seen mazes, demons, minotaurs, and everything else before. The few
interesting levels are those that are drastically varied (i.e.
Juiblex's Swamp.) I don't think we want to make Gehennom much
*harder*, otherwise people would get really annoyed after spending
hours with their character only to have it die midway through
Gehennom. I can't speak for anyone else, but I know that by that time,
I want a bit of a break. And we certainly don't want to make it
*longer*.

But what it seems Gehennom needs is some way in which it is
drastically different from the rest of the game -- in the scenery, at
least, or perhaps even the fundamental rules of reality. This is
*Hell*, after all! You should know, very clearly, that you're no
longer in the land of the living.

So after some thought, I came up with six possibilities. Hell could
be:

1) Mythos-specific
This would vary the scenery and monsters of Gehennom in a
class-specific way, like the Quest. Rangers, for example, would
descend into a Greek/Roman-style Hades, complete with Charon and
Cerberus and Pluto himself.
Advantages: makes ascensions of different classes much more
interesting.
Disadvantages: still would get monotonous after a while, if you play
enough characters of the same class. Spoilable.

2) Your worst nightmare
I liked this idea. This would really make it hell! After all, the
message says the "unspeakable cruelty and horror lurk down there,"
right?
This could be partially tailored to the class and partly to the
individual character. Valkyries, or other low-intelligence characters,
could find themselves facing mind flayers, confusing mazes filled with
hard-to-find secret doors and traps, etc. Rangers and other
lightweight fighters would be given mumaks, mastodons, minotaurs,
ettins, and other hard-to-kill beasts.
"Your worst nightmare" could also be made to fit the character
themselves, to some degree. If you don't have fire resistance (or lose
it along the way due to gremlin attack), you will be confronted by
hordes of fire elementals, red dragons, etc. If you have genocided
mind flayers, upon arriving in Gehennom you'll be beseiged by hordes
of the creatures. If you have low magic cancellation and are not
level-drain resistant, vampires, shades and so on will swoop in to
attack you. Etc., etc....
Advantages: would be highly character-specific; one would never
encounter the same Gehennom twice; gives players a real challenge by
finding their weaknesses; non-spoilable to some degree
Disadvantages: perhaps too difficult and frustrating; to some degree
increases the "scavenger hunt" nature of the game because a player
will want to ensure they have all the resistances (though this could
be offset by stepping up gremlin attacks)

3) The home of dead creatures

Where do all the monsters you kill in the main dungeon go when you
kill them? To Gehennom would be the logical answer! When you get to
hell, it could be full of all the creatures that have died during the
course of a game. They would be present in ghost/shade/skeleton/zombie
form, so one could have, for example, "a mind flayer zombie," or "a
mastodon skeleton". They would be slightly weaker and perhaps have
reduced attacks, but would also be given new capabilities (like
level-drain).
Creatures that were already dead (i.e. anything you "destroy" rather
than "kill") would not go to Gehennom. Once it's destroyed in its
undead form, it's gone for good.
An especially nasty consequence would be genocide scrolls. After all,
if you assume that all the members of a species (120, I believe?) are
alive somewhere and that a genocide scroll wipes them all out at once,
they would all go to Gehennom...waiting to get you once you came
along. (This would reduce the number of mind flayers/liches genocided,
I think...)
Some limitations would have to be put on this, of course. It would not
be fair for an undead Medusa to pop up unexpectedly and stone you
whenever she wants. And, at the other extreme, it would be pretty
useless for kraken zombies to be randomly placed all over the dungeon.
Advantages: reduces the overuse of genocide scrolls, causes you to
think about whether you really want to kill something or just evade
it, reducing "hack your way through the dungeon" style fighting
Disadvantages: nothing really new, doesn't change the scenery of
Gehennom, spoilable

4) A place where nothing stays dead

In contrast with the above idea, Gehennom could be a place where
nothing ever stays dead! Anything you kill in Gehennom and do not
utterly destroy (by eating, disintegration, tinning, sacrificing,
etc.) will come back to life sooner or later, troll-style. Oh, and
everything can follow you up/down levels.
This could make for a rather interesting (and fast-paced) game: you
run desperately down the levels, an increasing horde of monsters
following you as you go, until you get the Amulet -- at which point
you have to turn around and fight your way back through all of them
again. (I think they should stop short of following you out of
Gehennom, though. That would be a bit extreme.)
Advantages: speeds up trip through Gehennom, makes for more
interesting fighting, poses a challenge to even well-armoured fighters
Disadvantages: promotes melee fighting over other methods of killing
(as your wands, scrolls, arrows, potions etc. will get used up, but
your sword won't.) This could make life difficult for Tourists,
Wizards, Rangers, etc.

5) The Land of Chaos

A place where *everything* is unpredictable. Identities of objects
swap suddenly, turning your ring of conflict into adornment or your
sword into a dagger. Traps move from one location to another. Walls of
mazes shift. Weapons break unexpectedly. Chameleons and doppelgangers
are rampant. Objects you put down teleport unexpectedly to other
levels, polymorph, or even vanish entirely! In short, nothing stays
constant except for you (your abilities, stats and intrinsics.)
Advantages: makes for a much more difficult and exciting game; reduces
ability of players to mindlessly plow through; requires you go on
looking for new objects once in Gehennom (to replace those you've
lost)
Disadvantages: may increase YAADs (due to inconvenient changing of
your equipment at crucial moments); some may find it annoying and
infuriating.

6) Land of puzzles/riddles

In order to get through each level of Gehennom, you would have to
solve riddles or logic puzzles like the castle drawbridge. Some could
be spoilable, but it would be good if there were quite a few that
changed with every game (again, like the drawbridge) forcing the
player to think it out independently. Mazes would contain secret doors
and locations that could not be revealed by magic mapping. Perhaps
new, unidentifiable objects could appear, with unknown uses that the
player would have to figure out.
Advantages: new, never seen before; unspoilable; adds a different
dimension to the game
Disadvantages: some people may find it frustrating.

Well, those are my six ideas for redesigning Hell. So...any comments?

